 ## Overview
-Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping is a technique for computing self-shadowing in participating media by precomputing transmittance values from the light's perspective into a 3D texture, which is then sampled during the view-dependent ray march [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining how much light reaches a sample point through a volume without performing a secondary ray march toward the light for every sample [S3, unofficial]. It differs from [Shadow Volume Rendering](1556-shadow-volume-rendering.md) by storing discrete transmittance layers in a 3D texture rather than a full 3D geometric volume, and from [Deep Shadow Maps](2166-deep-shadow-maps.md) by storing discrete transmittance layers rather than hierarchical density curves [S2, unofficial].
+Ray Marching with Shadow Mapping is a technique for computing self-shadowing in participating media by precomputing transmittance values from the light's perspective into a 3D texture, which is then sampled during the view-dependent ray march [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently determining how much light reaches a sample point through a volume without performing a secondary ray march toward the light for every sample [S3, unofficial]. It differs from [Shadow Volume Rendering](1556-shadow-volume-rendering.md) by using a 3D texture stack indexed by light-space depth rather than a full 3D geometric volume, and from [Deep Shadow Maps](2166-deep-shadow-maps.md) by storing discrete transmittance layers rather than hierarchical density curves [S2, unofficial].
 
